{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c47d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a814-198f-7de0-9f8f-cc12fecf9427"},"sequence":153,"recorded_at":1780531400000301,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"c85271b6-2806-4b59-af83-bf3e3b19854f","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"c85271b6-2806-4b59-af83-bf3e3b19854f","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"c85271b6-2806-4b59-af83-bf3e3b19854f"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0a814-aa3b-7063-a6e5-5476a67c7a55"},"task_id":"01a0a814-aa3b-7063-a6e5-5476a67c7a55","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0a814-aa3b-7063-a6e5-5476a67c7a55","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/.agents/skills/project-management/references/change-request-lifecycle.md`.\n1|# Change Request Lifecycle Runbook\n2|\n3|Orchestrator-owned. An accepted Change Request is accepted work, not delivered\n4|work. This page states the order, the recovery paths, and the republish\n5|semantics that used to live only in an operator's head.\n6|\n7|The full typed contract — how a candidate is snapshotted, what\n8|`repository_delta` means, what a `stale` revision is, and every refusal you can\n9|hit — stays in\n10|[tracked background spawn](tracked-background-spawn.md), section\n11|`Change Requests, review, rework, and checkpoint`. This page is the order of\n12|operations on top of it.\n13|\n14|---\n15|\n16|## The Change Request lifecycle, in order\n17|\n18|1. **Accept.** The reviewer runs\n19|   `task-board m 'accept_cr(<ELEMENT-ID>, revision=<N>, evidence=<ELEMENT-ID>_review-verdict.md)'`.\n20|   The element moves to `integrating`. It is accepted, not delivered. Only the\n21|   reviewer run that was handed the revision may accept it.\n22|2. **Checkpoint immediately.** For every non-final leaf, run\n23|   `task-board worktree checkpoint <ELEMENT-ID>` as the next action after\n24|   acceptance. The accepted tree lands on the Story branch only, and the leaf\n25|   stays `integrating` until Story integration lands it on trunk. Delay here is\n26|   what makes the next producer's Change Request go `stale`.\n27|3. **Checkpoint refreshes the real index automatically.** Candidate capture\n28|   records (but never mutates) the real-index tree. Under the bound run's Story\n29|   lease and the candidate-captured index CAS, checkpoint replaces only that\n30|   captured state and independently verifies branch/HEAD/index/worktree\n31|   identity. A typed\n32|   `change_request_checkpoint_index_unsynced` refusal is retried after its named\n33|   lock, I/O, or ownership condition is resolved; do not use a broad manual\n34|   reset that can erase unreviewed staged state.\n35|4. **Final leaf: integrate, do not checkpoint.** `checkpoint` refuses the\n36|   Story's last open leaf with `change_request_final_leaf_checkpoint`, because\n37|   closing it would promote a Story whose branch has never reached trunk. Land\n38|   the Story with\n39|   `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID> --cr <ELEMENT-ID> --revision <N>`.\n40|5. **When `integrate` refuses, report the refusal.** `validation_not_configured`\n41|   (`BUG-260807-1o5092`, the as-shipped behavior) and\n42|   `integration_indeterminate` for a board that lives outside the control root\n43|   are blockers, not detours. Report the refusal as the blocker it is and land\n44|   only by the documented manual signed substitute — an isolated clone, signed\n45|   cherry-pick of the exact reviewed head, a platform review record, and a\n46|   fail-closed plain push. Never route around a typed refusal by moving board\n47|   files or the branch by hand.\n48|6. **A lost run is recovered by republish, never by re-running the producer.** A\n49|   run that died after producing content but before the board recorded it still\n50|   has its content on disk. Republish it with provenance. Re-running the\n51|   producer discards work that already exists and costs the run again.\n52|7. **A republish of an unchanged accepted tree is an annotation, not a new\n53|   revision.** A candidate whose `repository_delta` is byte-identical to the\n54|   accepted one, and whose acceptance still stands, is not a new candidate.\n55|   Recording it as a revision turns the revision count — the only rework\n56|   measure the board has — into process noise.\n57|8. **Never commit manually on the Story branch.** The managed worktree's tree is\n58|   published by the producer's handoff and landed by `checkpoint` or\n59|   `integrate`. A hand commit on that branch makes the accepted snapshot and the\n60|   branch head disagree, and the disagreement surfaces later as an unexplained\n61|   `stale` or an integration that lands the wrong tree. A `task_delta` handoff\n62|   now fails closed on it: the tip is compared to the workspace record's\n63|   `checkpoint_oid` before the validation suite runs and before anything is\n64|   written, and a disagreement is refused with\n65|   `change_request_candidate_committed_past_checkpoint`. The producer repairs it\n66|   inside its own run with `git reset --soft <checkpoint_oid>` — the work stays\n67|   in the worktree — and completes the handoff again.\n68|\n69|## Recovering a stranded accepted task_delta\n70|\n71|A final leaf whose accepted revision carries kind `task_delta` instead of\n72|`story_final` — published before the checkpoint-readiness repair, or while\n73|the board lived outside the launcher control root — cannot integrate:\n74|`worktree integrate` demands the Story's integration unit and refuses. The\n75|accepted revision is immutable history; recovery supersedes it, never edits\n76|it.\n77|\n78|1. **Release.** `task-board worktree invalidate-acceptance <STORY-ID> --cr\n79|   <ELEMENT-ID> --reason \"accepted task_delta no longer matches derived\n80|   story_final\"`. This is admitted only when the kind re-derived from the\n81|   board actually differs from the recorded one; the accepted revision file\n82|   stays byte-identical and the leaf returns to rework. A record whose kind\n83|   still matches is refused — there is nothing to recover, look elsewhere.\n84|2. **Republish.** The rework producer run publishes the next revision, which\n85|   now derives `story_final` from the bound checkpoint loader.\n86|3. **Accept.** The reviewer accepts the new revision independently:\n87|   `task-board m 'accept_cr(<ELEMENT-ID>, revision=<N+1>,\n88|   evidence=<ELEMENT-ID>_review-verdict.md)'`.\n89|4. **Integrate.** `task-board worktree integrate <STORY-ID> --cr\n90|   <ELEMENT-ID> --revision <N+1>`, which closes the final leaf and co-closes\n91|   the checkpointed sibling and the Story.\n92|\n93|## A revision that will never be integrated\n94|\n95|Two shapes end a Change Request revision without a reviewer verdict, and they\n96|are different facts.\n97|\n98|### An empty delta does not block the Story\n99|\n100|A revision whose `repository_delta` is `empty` — an analyst or research run that\n101|changed no repository file — is created `ready` like any other, because\n102|emptiness is an attribute of the candidate and never a state. What changed with\n103|`BUG-260906-37s3ps` is what that revision does to everybody else: while it sits\n104|`ready` or `reviewing`, it no longer refuses a sibling producer with\n105|`change_request_sibling_producer_blocked`.\n106|\n107|The gate's argument is contamination — the new producer writes into the same\n108|Story worktree the pending candidate was snapshotted from — and an empty\n109|candidate has no repository work product for those writes to reach. Its\n110|checkpoint writes no commit, and its integration is a no-op. Before the fix a\n111|research-only run deadlocked its whole Story: nothing was routed at a candidate\n112|with nothing to review, and every later producer was refused with a blocker\n113|nobody could clear.\n114|\n115|The exemption is scoped, and the scope is deliberate:\n116|\n117|| Blocking state | `repository_delta=present` | `repository_delta=empty` |\n118|| --- | --- | --- |\n119|| `ready`, `reviewing` | refuses siblings | **admits siblings** |\n120|| `draft`, `changes_requested`, `stale`, `conflicted` | refuses siblings | refuses siblings |\n121|\n122|The bottom row expects a REPUBLISH, and the next snapshot of the shared worktree\n123|would pick up the sibling's writes. Emptiness today says nothing about the\n124|revision that replaces it. A revision with no readable `repository_delta` —\n125|legacy, or from a newer build — refuses: a value this build cannot read is not\n126|evidence that the candidate is empty.\n127|\n128|The revision is still fully auditable. It is not deleted, not hidden, and not\n129|given a special state; it simply stops being a blocker.\n130|\n131|### Retiring a revision: `withdraw_cr`\n132|\n133|An empty delta is not the only revision that never reaches integration. A\n134|candidate can be superseded, abandoned, or published by a run whose deliverable\n135|turned out to belong somewhere else. Before `BUG-260906-37s3ps` there was no\n136|route for that at all: `accept_cr` needs a reviewer verdict and a merged\n137|checklist, `worktree abort` discards the entire workspace, and republishing only\n138|produces another revision in the same unresolved state.\n139|\n140|```bash\n141|task-board m 'withdraw_cr(<ELEMENT-ID>, revision=<N>, reason=\"why this revision will never be integrated\")'\n142|```\n143|\n144|It moves the revision to `aborted`, which is terminal on both predicates that\n145|read the state: it stops refusing sibling producers, and it stops withholding\n146|the Story workspace's base convergence. The revision file and its ledger row\n147|stay readable.\n148|\n149|Four things it refuses, and why:\n150|\n151|- **A revision that is `accepted`, `checkpointing`, `checkpointed`,\n152|  `integrating` or `integrated`** — `change_request_state_conflict`. Those\n153|  carry a reviewer's attestation, or commits that are already on the Story\n154|  branch or in trunk. Recording one as never integrated would make the board\n155|  disagree with git history.\n156|- **A missing or whitespace-only `reason`** —\n157|  `change_request_withdraw_reason_missing`. The ledger row is the only surviving\n158|  explanation of why the revision exists and was never integrated.\n159|- **A call from inside a tracked spawn run** —\n160|  `change_request_withdraw_unauthorized`. Withdrawal is an ORCHESTRATOR route.\n161|  A producer refused by `change_request_sibling_producer_blocked` must not clear\n162|  its own blocker, and a producer handed `changes_requested` must not retire the\n163|  rework signal instead of answering it.\n164|- **A revision number that is not the current one** —\n165|  `change_request_revision_conflict`, the same CAS every other transition takes.\n166|\n167|`withdraw_cr` deliberately does NOT move the board element. Where the element\n168|goes next — back to development, `blocked`, or closed as a duplicate — is a\n169|routing decision the orchestrator makes with information the mutation does not\n170|have; make it explicitly, in a separate call.\n171|\n172|## Two same-class findings mean a missing gate\n173|\n174|When two consecutive reviews of one leaf raise the same finding class, stop\n175|routing another rework revision. Spawn one task to add a gate for the class — a\n176|conformance vector set, a checker, a mutant harness — and let the next revision\n177|be reviewed against the gate rather than against a reviewer's memory. The\n178|reviewer's `repeat-of:` verdict field is the signal to route on.\n179|\n180|## Recovery surfaces\n181|\n182|- Stopped or refused integration: `task-board worktree transaction show <STORY-ID>`,\n183|  then the disposition surface it names.\n184|- A leaf whose handoff guard refused for artifact naming: attach the correctly\n185|  named `<ELEMENT-ID>_<slug>.<ext>` outcome resource and re-run the handoff. Do\n186|  not respawn the producer to satisfy a naming rule.","final_result":true}}}
