{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c481","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a814-198f-7de0-9f8f-cc12fecf9427"},"sequence":155,"recorded_at":1780531400000305,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"c85271b6-2806-4b59-af83-bf3e3b19854f","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"c85271b6-2806-4b59-af83-bf3e3b19854f","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"c85271b6-2806-4b59-af83-bf3e3b19854f"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0a814-aa63-7f40-839e-63cc88b417d2"},"task_id":"01a0a814-aa63-7f40-839e-63cc88b417d2","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0a814-aa63-7f40-839e-63cc88b417d2","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/.agents/skills/project-management/references/tracked-background-spawn.md`.\n1|# Tracked Background Spawn\n2|\n3|Canonical operator-facing contract for tracked background spawn runs. Covers `task-board spawn`, observation, control, fallback manual launch patterns, failure modes, and working habits.\n4|\n5|**Primary interface:** `task-board spawn TASK-ID --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` — wraps an explicit agent selection with automatic board integration.\n6|\n7|**Context plane:** with `features.context_sharing` on, a spawn also resolves a\n8|shared context. No flag means deterministic auto selection; repeated `--context`\n9|names the primary and its supplementals in binding order; `--no-context`\n10|suppresses the lookup and is mutually exclusive with `--context`;\n11|`--register-context project|role|task` is the only publication opt-in, and\n12|`project` requires the canonical `context-builder` role. Selection is gated on\n13|what this machine can actually serve: a `ready` record whose provider handle is\n14|not here — published by a colleague, or evicted by `materialization_idle_ttl` —\n15|is dropped from auto selection with a `context_not_materialized` rejection and\n16|the run starts cold, while an explicitly named `--context` is refused instead.\n17|The registry record is never mutated by that verdict; the snapshot is intact on\n18|the machine that holds it. The child receives\n19|`TASK_BOARD_CONTEXT_ID` for its own writable fork and nothing else. A run that\n20|holds a writable child executes inside a Session Manager session — a prompt-mode\n21|launch would inherit nothing — and it needs no delivery goal to do so: the\n22|context plane owns that run on its own, exactly as the goal plane owns a\n23|`--goal-scope` run. Inspect the decision before launching with\n24|`task-board q 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=ROLE, agent=PROVIDER)'`.\n25|**Canonical scope:** README and SKILL.md should summarize spawn behavior, not redefine it. For selection semantics see [`.specs/spawn-model-selection.md`](../.specs/spawn-model-selection.md); for durable runner/wait semantics see [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](../.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).\n26|\n27|---\n28|\n29|## Session Manager owner boundary\n30|\n31|`task-board codex` and `task-board claude` are thin clients of the board-keyed\n32|Task-board Session Manager. `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic and renders\n33|the non-launching primary-session plan. The manager owns provider lifetime and\n34|native reflection for both parent and spawned owners.\n35|\n36|| Owner | Ledger slot | Provider session |\n37|| --- | --- | --- |\n38|| Primary wrapper | `parent/primary` | Manager-held Codex app-server/thread or Claude PTY |\n39|| Tracked spawn | `run/RUN-*` | Distinct manager-held provider host for that run |\n40|\n41|The run receives no manager endpoint, provider URL, thread/session identity, or\n42|credential. Attach reconciles the exact v2 owner head before the assignment\n43|turn. Provider parity covers requirement delivery, directives, recovery,\n44|handoff evidence, acceptance, and audit; provider mechanics remain private to\n45|the Codex and Claude host adapters.\n46|\n47|---\n48|\n49|## What Is Tracked Background Spawn\n50|\n51|Tracked background spawn starts a real agent runtime without blocking the\n52|parent/orchestrator shell. Ordinary prompt-mode runs use `claude -p`, `codex\n53|exec`, or Qwen stream-JSON; goal-bound Codex/Claude owners execute inside a\n54|manager-held session. Every run gets a durable `RUN-...` handle, persisted\n55|state, replayable events, watcher output, a wait barrier, directives, logs, and\n56|completion hooks.\n57|\n58|The parent agent is the active Orchestrator role (`orchestrator` / legacy `coordinator`). It creates or selects board tasks, spawns child runs, watches their progress, sends directives when needed, and reads child outcome resources for routing. It should not turn into the worker or reviewer just because a task is small.\n59|\n60|**Use cases:**\n61|- Delegate implementation to a separate agent process with its own context window\n62|- Run agents on different model families (Claude vs OpenAI) for diversity\n63|- Scale beyond runtime-native helper slots while preserving board tracking\n64|- Preserve nested spawn capability across parent/child agent processes\n65|\n66|---\n67|\n68|## Spawning via `task-board spawn` (Recommended)\n69|\n70|The `spawn` command is the default way to launch tracked background runs. It handles assignment, prompt composition, persistence, logging, events, and completion hooks automatically.\n71|\n72|**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex, Qwen, Claude and Muse additionally require a model-compatible `--reasoning-effort`, each from its own effort vocabulary. Agy and Gemini reject the flag outright, and so does `muse-spark-1.2-contributor` — Muse is per-model: `muse-spark-1.3-contributor` and its `muse-spark` alias REQUIRE one of `high`/`xhigh`/`max` and refuse a launch without it, the 1.2 row takes none, and the versioned-short `muse-spark-1.3` is registered by nothing and is not spawnable ([Muse readiness](../.specs/spawn-model-selection.md#muse-readiness)). Codex is per-model at the head of its lineup: `gpt-6-astra` takes one of `low`/`medium`/`high`/`xhigh`/`max`/`ultra` and refuses the legacy `minimal`, and the floating short `astra` is its launched alias taking the same six ([Astra readiness](../.specs/spawn-model-selection.md#astra-readiness)). The role drives agent identity, skills, status lifecycle, and definition of done. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults of its own. Role resolution precedes ceiling lookup and board access: boundary whitespace and case are normalized, the legacy `coordinator` spelling shares the `orchestrator` policy identity, and path-like or unknown names fail closed with typed errors.\n73|\n74|**A project may waive `--model` for one role.** `spawn.ceilings.<provider>.roles.<role>.default_model` and `.default_reasoning_effort` are per-role selection defaults: they seed only the dimensions the caller omitted, an explicit flag always wins, and the seeded pair is resolved by exactly the same ceiling that bounds an explicit one. A ceiling is not a default — it bounds a selection the caller already made and cannot produce one — so configuring a bound never removes the flag. See [per-role model preferences](task-board-config.md#per-role-model-preferences) for the rules, the refusals, and the `spawn-policy-v2` rationale gate that a default does not waive.\n75|\n76|Before spawning, resolve only the target policy:\n77|\n78|```bash\n79|task-board q --format compact 'project_config(view=spawn-preflight, role=developer, agent=codex)'\n80|```\n81|\n82|The projection returns the effective provider policy/schedule, target role\n83|ceiling, target role selection default, selection mode, composition status, and\n84|local queue capacity without auth/session/version-control data.\n85|`resolved_role_default.spawns_without_manual_selection` reports whether that role\n86|can be spawned for that provider without `--model`. Query `models(agent=<selected-agent>)` only\n87|when the registered set is needed to resolve a model. Full `project_config()`\n88|is the audit/debug surface.\n89|\n90|## Provider System And UTC Schedule\n91|\n92|`spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is the canonical provider policy. It is an\n93|enforced tagged union: `exclusive` contains one provider, while `mixed`\n94|contains at least two unique normalized providers. Explicit `--agent` wins over\n95|affinity only inside that set. Otherwise exclusive pins its provider and mixed\n96|keeps the current Codex/Claude runtime affinity only when the set allows it.\n97|Out-of-set explicit providers or affinities are refused before task lookup,\n98|assignment, prompt/run creation, or runner launch.\n99|\n100|```json\n101|{\n102|  \"spawn\": {\n103|    \"preferred_agentic_system\": {\n104|      \"mixed\": [\"claude\", \"codex\"]\n105|    },\n106|    \"schedule\": {\n107|      \"windows\": [\n108|        {\n109|          \"start_utc\": \"17:00\",\n110|          \"end_utc\": \"03:00\",\n111|          \"preferred_agentic_system\": {\n112|            \"exclusive\": \"qwen\"\n113|          }\n114|        }\n115|      ]\n116|    }\n117|  }\n118|}\n119|```\n120|\n121|Schedule bounds are UTC `HH:MM`, start-inclusive/end-exclusive, and disjoint.\n122|Later-to-earlier bounds cross midnight; equal or overlapping bounds are\n123|invalid. An active window replaces the base provider system before provider\n124|and registered-model validation. Outside every window, the base system applies;\n125|if no base exists, ordinary runtime affinity remains unrestricted. The selected\n126|provider's ceiling is resolved only after this step.\n127|\n128|`project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is the currently effective\n129|`{mode, allowed_providers, source, config_key}` projection.\n130|`project_config().spawn.schedule` separately reports `timezone: \"UTC\"`, all\n131|normalized windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system`.\n132|Legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` remains readable and projects to an effective\n133|exclusive system, but startup diagnoses it with the canonical replacement.\n134|The misspelling `preferred_agent_system` is not an alias.\n135|\n136|## Project MCP Launch Composition\n137|\n138|Tracked children inherit the project's enabled MCP servers through the versioned `agents-infra.child-launch-composition` contract. This capability is default-on:\n139|\n140|```json\n141|{\n142|  \"spawn\": {\n143|    \"launch_composition\": {\n144|      \"enabled\": true\n145|    }\n146|  }\n147|}\n148|```\n149|\n150|Omitting either `launch_composition` or `enabled` means `true`; explicit `false` is the only opt-out. Local mode reads `spawn.launch_composition.enabled`. Remote mode reads `remote.spawn.launch_composition.enabled`, with the existing whole-policy root fallback only when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null`. A present remote policy with the field omitted defaults on instead of inheriting the root field. `project_config().spawn.launch_composition` reports the effective boolean, active dotted key, contract, schema version `1`, and `bare_with_evidence` degradation policy without invoking infra.\n151|\n152|After agent selection and work-directory resolution, each ordinary prompt-mode\n153|spawn invokes the non-launching child composition command **once**, for the\n154|directory the child will actually run in:\n155|\n156|```bash\n157|agents-infra compose --agent qwen --project \"$EXECUTION_ROOT\" --schema-version 1 --json\n158|```\n159|\n160|`$EXECUTION_ROOT` is the Story worktree when the run has one, and the control\n161|root otherwise. Composing the control root and then running the child elsewhere\n162|would hand it MCP servers scoped to a directory it never opens, so the project\n163|follows the execution root rather than the observation root. Task-board then\n164|validates that the envelope names that same canonical project.\n165|\n166|Task-board validates the contract identity, canonical project, selected provider, required metadata, and the complete provider allowlist before using any prefix. Codex accepts only `-c mcp_servers.<name>.(url|bearer_token_env_var|command|args)=...` pairs. Claude and Qwen accept only one `--mcp-config` JSON value rooted at `mcpServers`, with the contract's HTTP or stdio shapes. Any extra top-level flag, primary-session policy, unknown field, server mismatch, malformed JSON, or literal Bearer [REDACTED] rejects the whole prefix; task-board never salvages individual arguments.\n167|\n168|The accepted prefix is prepended at the provider's top-level boundary for\n169|ordinary prompt-mode launches. A goal-bound Codex/Claude owner instead requires\n170|`agents-infra compose --mode primary-session`; task-board validates and\n171|persists that exact managed-host plan for the Session Manager. Qwen does not\n172|support goal-bound owners. Restart, reroute, and autonomous recovery successors\n173|retain the plan and rebind only the new `RUN-*` owner context.\n174|\n175|| Condition | Child launch | Sanitized status |\n176||---|---|---|\n177|| Explicit opt-out | Bare | `disabled` |\n178|| Valid prefix with MCP servers | Composed | `applied` |\n179|| Valid empty MCP set | Bare-equivalent | `empty` |\n180|| `agents-infra` missing | Bare | `degraded_tool_missing` |\n181|| Old/pre-contract command | Bare | `degraded_contract_unavailable` |\n182|| Unsupported contract/schema | Bare | `degraded_unsupported_schema` |\n183|| Recognized composition failure | Bare | `degraded_compose_failed` |\n184|| Malformed, mismatched, or disallowed payload | Bare | `degraded_invalid_payload` |\n185|\n186|Degradation is fail-open only for this optional capability: it never weakens or rewrites board-owned safety flags. `spawn --json` returns the safe summary under `launchComposition`; task notes and durable run state/events carry the same snake-case summary. The private manifest alone stores the validated MCP argv snapshot needed by the detached runner. Public evidence never contains argv payloads, URLs, commands, command arguments, headers, external stderr, or environment values.\n187|\n188|`bearer_token_env_var` is a name reference, not a value lookup. Task-board and agents-infra do not resolve it during composition. The child inherits unrelated caller environment variables through the existing provider filters, while evidence retains only referenced variable names. Do not put token values in task prompts, notes, resources, manifests, events, logs, or command diagnostics.\n189|\n190|## Story Worktree Isolation\n191|\n192|A repository-mutating producer spawn does not run in the shared checkout. It\n193|runs in a Git worktree scoped to its containing Story, so concurrent producers\n194|in independent Stories stop clobbering one working tree and each Story's commits\n195|stay clean and buildable on their own.\n196|\n197|| Concern | Value |\n198||---|---|\n199|| Isolation unit | One managed worktree and branch per Story |\n200|| Branch | `task-board/story/<STORY-ID>`, forked from trunk, short-lived |\n201|| Path | `<control-root>/.temp/<STORY-ID>/worktree` |\n202|| Delivery model | Trunk-based; the complete review-accepted Story lands directly on trunk |\n203|| Parallelism | Independent Stories run concurrently; one exclusive lease serializes writers inside a Story |\n204|| Ownership | task-board owns Git state; provider-native worktree flags are never used |\n205|\n206|Delivery is trunk-based development with short-lived Story branches and no\n207|partial landing: an internal Task checkpoint never reaches trunk on its own, and\n208|there is no feature flag that turns isolation off. How a Story lands as commits\n209|is the commit-owning mover's own call — see\n210|[statuses](statuses.md#commit-confirmation-for-accepted-work).\n211|Configure trunk with `spawn.worktree_isolation.integration_base_branch`, or\n212|omit it for remote-neutral discovery: the one branch a remote HEAD and\n213|matching tracking config jointly and uniquely name — discovery reads every\n214|configured remote and never assumes one is called `origin` — or, only when\n215|the repository has no remote at all, the control root's current non-detached\n216|branch. Before the selected branch is trusted for provisioning, checkpoint, or\n217|integration, its configured upstream (when it has one) is fetched and compared\n218|against the local tip by exact OID: an ahead or diverged local tip, or an\n219|unreadable/ambiguous authority, refuses rather than proceeding from unproven\n220|state. A branch with positively no upstream configured uses its own local OID\n221|directly, with no fetch. See the\n222|[canonical config reference](task-board-config.md#story-worktree-isolation)\n223|for the full discovery order and refusal table.\n224|\n225|### Control root versus execution root\n226|\n227|The old single work directory is split, and the split is what keeps a wave\n228|observable from one place:\n229|\n230|- **Control root** — the project root. Owns the authoritative board, roles,\n231|  prompts, logwork, `.temp/spawn-runs`, directives, scheduler locks, the\n232|  workspace registry, and `spawn.max_parallel`. Every observation command reads\n233|  here.\n234|- **Execution root** — `<control-root>/.temp/<STORY-ID>/worktree`. The child's\n235|  working directory and the project passed to agents-infra composition, which\n236|  runs once, for the execution root.\n237|\n238|The run manifest keeps `work_dir` as a backward-compatible control-root alias\n239|and adds explicit `control_root` and `execution_root`. RUN state and events\n240|carry the Story id and a **sanitized** workspace identity — the\n241|control-root-relative path plus opaque workspace/repository ids. The absolute\n242|execution path exists only in the private manifest under the gitignored\n243|`.temp/spawn-runs/` tree.\n244|\n245|That public identity reports `protected_trunk_oid` only when the durable\n246|workspace record says `authority_source: fetched_upstream`; the value is the\n247|recorded `upstream_oid`. A legacy record with no authority source, or a record\n248|from `cached_upstream` or `local_no_upstream`, remains compatible but reports\n249|`protected_trunk_oid: null`. `selected_base_oid` is workspace-base evidence,\n250|not protected-trunk proof, and is never used as a fallback.\n251|\n252|Stacked-base authority is deliberately not another workspace-registry field.\n253|`TASK-260831-35qu9f` owns that future per-Story file protocol under\n254|`.temp/worktrees/`: `<STORY-ID>.grant.json`,\n255|`<STORY-ID>.provision-intent.json`, and immutable\n256|`<STORY-ID>.grant.<issued-at>.consumed.json` or `.revoked.json` terminal\n257|records. Keeping this grant/intent lifecycle separate prevents a scoped,\n258|expiring authorization from becoming ambient authority on every `Ref` read.\n259|\n260|Children are pointed at the authoritative board explicitly, with an absolute\n261|`TASK_BOARD_DIR`. A linked worktree contains its own `.task-board` checkout\n262|copy; that copy is a checkout artifact, not the board. Do not run `task-board`\n263|against it, and do not override `TASK_BOARD_DIR`.\n264|\n265|Restart, reroute, and recovery successors inherit the workspace rather than\n266|provisioning a second one. For a workspace-bound run the control root and the\n267|execution root are one pair: a reroute `--workdir` override is **rejected**\n268|rather than applied, because the registry record and the Story lease live under\n269|the control root while the child writes in the execution root, and moving one\n270|without the other would let two runs each believe they hold the lease for the\n271|same worktree. Respawn the Story instead. A run with no Story workspace still\n272|accepts the override.\n273|\n274|### Control-root cleanliness\n275|\n276|The control-root working tree carries board state under `.task-board` and the\n277|operator-owned project config, and nothing else. Every repository source, test,\n278|documentation or workflow change is made in a Story worktree and reaches trunk\n279|through a pull request. Apply without judgement: a dirty path is allowed only\n280|when it is under `.task-board/` or when git itself reports it ignored; any\n281|other dirty path refuses the spawn.\n282|\n283|The naive rule — \"the control root must be clean\" — is wrong, because the\n284|authoritative board must live in the control root and a linked worktree\n285|`.task-board` copy is a checkout artifact rather than the board. Board writes\n286|in the control root are correct by design, and `git status` in a live control\n287|root routinely shows `.task-board/**` as dirty. What must never appear there\n288|is a repository change outside those two planes.\n289|\n290|One-command check, run in the control root. Expected output is empty (no\n291|lines). Any printed path is an offending non-board, non-ignored dirty path:\n292|\n293|```bash\n294|git status --porcelain=v1 --untracked-files=all -z -- . ':!.task-board' | tr '\\0' '\\n' | sed -n 's/^...//p' | grep -v '^$' | while IFS= read -r p; do git check-ignore -q -- \"$p\" || printf '%s\\n' \"$p\"; done\n295|```\n296|\n297|```\n298|(empty)\n299|```\n300|\n301|`planSpawnStoryWorkspace` enforces the same rule through\n302|`worktree.RequireCleanControlRoot` before any lease, branch, worktree, record,\n303|assignment, prompt, or RUN state. A violation refuses with\n304|`worktree_control_root_dirty` naming the offending paths in `paths`. An\n305|unreadable `git status` or `git check-ignore` refuses with\n306|`worktree_git_command_failed`: unknown is not clean. Ignored admission is\n307|decided by `git check-ignore` against the trunk's own ignore rules, not by a\n308|second hardcoded filename list, so the operator config (`/task-board.config.json`,\n309|gitignored on trunk) is admitted by the same rule that excludes every other\n310|ignored path.\n311|\n312|### Which runs get a workspace\n313|\n314|| Run | Workspace |\n315||---|---|\n316|| `orchestrator` / `coordinator` | No — stays in the control root |\n317|| `task_class=metadata` | No — control-plane only, must not edit repository files |\n318|| `code`, `docs`, `research` producers | Yes |\n319|| Reviewer of such a leaf | Yes — the same workspace, holding the same lease so the candidate cannot drift during review |\n320|| Element with no containing Story | No — there is no Story-scoped workspace to provision |\n321|\n322|The first three rows are decided facts about what the run *is*, so they produce\n323|no workspace and the spawn proceeds in the control root. A repository question is\n324|not one of them. When a run in the \"Yes\" rows has no repository to isolate in,\n325|the spawn **refuses** rather than quietly falling back to the shared checkout:\n326|\n327|| Control root | Result |\n328||---|---|\n329|| Not a Git working tree | `worktree_not_a_git_repository`, before assignment, prompt, RUN, or child |\n330|| Git state unreadable (git missing, probe failed) | `worktree_git_command_failed` — unknown, not absent |\n331|\n332|Both refusals are evaluated after the §9.1 local-board capability precondition,\n333|so a resolved remote board still reports `worktree_isolation_requires_local_board`\n334|first and an operator is never sent to fix a repository that was never the\n335|blocker.\n336|\n337|### Scheduling\n338|\n339|A run waiting for a Story lease stays `queued` and **consumes no\n340|`spawn.max_parallel` slot**, and it does not block independent Stories queued\n341|behind it. Admission takes Story eligibility first and a global slot second, and\n342|releases both on every terminal path.\n343|\n344|### Story lifecycle boundaries\n345|\n346|**Do not attach new repository work to an integrated Story.** Its managed\n347|workspace is spent and its base is frozen at the pre-integration trunk, so a\n348|new producer would build from stale authority and replay work that already\n349|landed. This happened when 15 of 22 paths in a post-integration candidate\n350|duplicated paths already on trunk. Create a new Story and workspace from current\n351|trunk for the new work.\n352|\n353|**Do not land a trunk change while a Story is in flight.** Every in-flight Story\n354|then needs a base refresh before its tree can land, multiplying revalidation and\n355|conflict risk across the whole wave. In the observed failure, trunk and the\n356|Story had independently fixed the same defect on the same config line, so the\n357|required replay aborted with a conflict. Sequence trunk landing around active\n358|Stories rather than forcing every Story to absorb the moving base.\n359|\n360|### Change Requests, review, rework, and checkpoint\n361|\n362|A producer that hands off inside a managed workspace publishes a **Change\n363|Request**: a revisioned record attached to the existing Task/Bug/Story, not a new\n364|board element. It carries the branch, the base and candidate tree OIDs, a binary\n365|patch resource with its SHA-256, the changed-path list, and an explicit\n366|`repository_delta` of `present` or `empty`.\n367|\n368|The candidate is snapshotted through a temporary alternate Git index, so tracked\n369|changes, deletions, and untracked non-ignored files are all captured **without\n370|touching the managed worktree's own index**. Every `.task-board/**` entry is then\n371|restored from the base tree, which is why a `changed_paths` list can never\n372|contain a board path — the authoritative board is the control root, and a\n373|worktree's `.task-board` copy is a checkout artifact. Paths dropped by that rule\n374|are recorded as `dropped_board_paths` and shown by `worktree status` and in the\n375|reviewer's prompt. A non-empty list is a diagnostic, usually a `git add -A` in\n376|the child, not a failure.\n377|\n378|Construction runs for **every** producer completion in a managed workspace,\n379|whether or not the run carries a delivery goal. Managed workspace activation\n380|turns on role, task class, containing Story and local board alone, so an ordinary\n381|`task-board spawn TASK-... --role developer --background` gets an isolated\n382|worktree — and the half of the feature that makes that worktree reviewable\n383|activates on exactly the same conditions.\n384|\n385|It runs **after** the spawn completion artifact guard, and on the same two facts:\n386|the board reached the run's end status, and the run attached a new or updated\n387|task-scoped outcome artifact. A run that attached nothing is reverted to its\n388|producer status by that guard and leaves no Change Request at all. A read that\n389|FAILS is reported as a failure and never as \"did not hand off\".\n390|\n391|A workspace-claiming producer that publishes nothing says why, on the run's event\n392|log and in a board note: `handoff_unsatisfied`, with the detail — the board is\n393|not at the end status, or no task-scoped outcome artifact was attached.\n394|\n395|The other two reasons are recorded nowhere, on one rule: a reason that is true of\n396|every run of its class says nothing about the run in front of you, and repeating\n397|it on every completion buries the case the reporting exists for. An un-isolated\n398|run skips with `no_managed_workspace`, which is true of every un-isolated run. A\n399|reviewer-archetype run skips with `not_a_producer`, which is true of every\n400|reviewer run — a reviewer judges a candidate rather than publishing one, so\n401|noting it would append a line describing correct behaviour to the element's\n402|progress on every single review cycle.\n403|\n404|`repository_delta=empty` means the producer changed no repository file. It is an\n405|attribute, never a state and never terminal: the Change Request is `ready` like\n406|any other, a reviewer **is** spawned, and the injected prompt says in plain terms\n407|that nothing changed and lists the outcome resources that were produced. Deciding\n408|whether that was correct is a judgement about intent versus delivery, which is\n409|what the reviewer archetype is for.\n410|\n411|**The kind is derived from the board, never declared.** A Change Request is\n412|`task_delta` or `story_final`, and the second is what `worktree integrate`\n413|accepts as the Story's integration unit. Publication asks the board whether\n414|completing this leaf would also close its Story — the same question\n415|`worktree checkpoint` asks to REFUSE that leaf, through the same call — so the\n416|leaf one command declines is exactly the leaf the other treats as the Story's\n417|unit. Every indeterminate answer refuses the publication: no board access, an\n418|unreadable board, or an element the board does not contain means the question was\n419|never answered, and answering `task_delta` on a failed read would leave the Story\n420|permanently unintegrable with nothing recording why.\n421|\n422|**The base follows the kind.** A `task_delta` is measured from the Story branch\n423|tip, where earlier leaves already checkpointed. A `story_final` is measured from\n424|the workspace record's **current base OID**, initially the exact selected base\n425|OID produced by upstream freshness resolution and later advanced only by a\n426|successful final-leaf refresh or by final publication after it proves selected\n427|upstream authority, checkpoint, managed branch, and checked-out HEAD have one\n428|consistent provenance. Publication accepts either the unchanged checkpoint with\n429|an uncommitted candidate in the worktree, or a branch/HEAD exactly one direct,\n430|single-parent commit past that checkpoint when the commit tree equals the tree\n431|independently snapshotted from the selected base and present worktree. It is\n432|never recomputed through the local trunk ref: that ref may remain behind the\n433|upstream that provisioning fetched, which would expand the patch to\n434|already-merged predecessor work. Any OID/tree/provenance mismatch refuses before\n435|validation or partial Change Request persistence. The candidate tree is a full\n436|snapshot, so the recorded base is also the stable input to later re-parenting\n437|when trunk moves again.\n438|\n439|**A Story has at most one non-checkpointed producer candidate.** The worktree is\n440|Story-scoped and the snapshotter has no durable per-task path ownership model.\n441|Before a producer starts, spawn therefore scans the Story's Change Requests and\n442|refuses `change_request_sibling_producer_blocked` when another element owns a\n443|`draft`, `ready`, `reviewing`, `changes_requested`, `stale`, `conflicted`, or\n444|`accepted` revision: `accepted` blocks until `checkpointed`, and `checkpointed`\n445|does not block. The refusal names the blocking task and Change Request and is\n446|checked in two places: in the spawn command before provisioning (and again\n447|after lease admission there), and in the runner's slot walk after lease\n448|admission and before `slot_claimed`, which is the only path that closes the\n449|concurrent-spawn race for queued runs. Reviewer-archetype runs bypass the\n450|producer gate, as does the integration run bound to the element's own accepted\n451|revision, and the same element may start rework for its own revision. A failed\n452|Change Request store read is a refusal that keeps a queued run queued and is\n453|never treated as no sibling.\n454|\n455|One exception, added by `BUG-260906-37s3ps`: a `ready` or `reviewing` revision\n456|whose `repository_delta` is `empty` does NOT refuse a sibling. It carries no\n457|repository work product for the new producer's writes to contaminate, its\n458|checkpoint creates no commit, and blocking on it deadlocked any Story whose\n459|analyst run published a zero-path candidate no reviewer was ever routed at. The\n460|four states that expect a REPUBLISH — `draft`, `changes_requested`, `stale`,\n461|`conflicted` — keep refusing whatever their delta is, because the next snapshot\n462|of the shared worktree would pick up the sibling's writes. A record with no\n463|readable `repository_delta` refuses too. See\n464|[change request lifecycle](change-request-lifecycle.md).\n465|\n466|**The producer runs the landing suite and records tree-bound evidence.** When\n467|`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` is configured, publication runs it\n468|in the Story worktree and records `validation` keyed to the published candidate\n469|tree. The suite runs against the WORKTREE while the evidence claims a TREE, so\n470|the candidate is recomputed afterwards and compared; a suite that moved the\n471|worktree — a build that drops artifacts, a codegen step, a formatter — yields\n472|**no** evidence rather than evidence keyed to a tree it never saw. A failing\n473|suite is fail-sticky at its first non-zero command and cannot publish a `ready`\n474|candidate: the immutable revision is immediately `changes_requested`, its\n475|bounded transcript is attached as a task-scoped system resource, and validation\n476|records the one-based failed command index/total plus the real exit status. The\n477|producer run follows the existing non-success/recovery-successor path with a\n478|retry step naming that resource. A command that could not run at all is never\n479|recorded as a pass. Passing and explicitly empty configured suites retain the\n480|ready/success path. See\n481|[how the evidence binds](#validation-binds-to-the-tree-that-lands).\n482|\n483|**Write validation commands for the managed worktree they actually run in.**\n484|The validation suite runs inside that worktree alongside gitignored scratch and\n485|reviewer probes, so repository-wide filesystem walks and index comparisons can\n486|inspect the wrong population. One project hit all three failures in the same\n487|suite:\n488|\n489|| Gate | Naive command | Observed failure |\n490|| --- | --- | --- |\n491|| Generated-file freshness | `go generate && git diff --exit-code -- <file>` | `git diff` compared the producer's intentionally unstaged file against the index and refused a correct candidate tree. |\n492|| JSON validity | `find . -name '*.json' \\| xargs ...` | `find` walked 287 files including scratch, then `xargs` failed with `command line cannot be assembled, too long`. |\n493|| Formatting | `test -z \"$(gofmt -l .)\"` | `gofmt` descended into `.temp/` and reported a reviewer's probe as a product formatting failure. |\n494|\n495|Enumerate repository content with `git ls-files -co --exclude-standard` (use\n496|`-z` and a NUL-aware consumer when filenames are passed onward), then filter to\n497|the relevant suffixes or directories. For a generator, compare the selected\n498|file's before/after content rather than comparing the generated result to the\n499|index. These shapes keep tracked files and non-ignored candidate additions in\n500|scope while excluding the worktree's ignored operational scratch.\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
