versus child aggregate `development`; raw output is attached without calling it clean.\n47|- REVIEW REWORK 9: CR revision 8 correctly proved that unchanged `NativeDependencies/manifest.json` locks did not protect actual HEV XCFramework bytes. The immutable binding now carries the exact 16-file HEV path/SHA-256 map; production validation requires the checked-in lock map, complete artifact file set, and every actual file digest to match at stable `REPOSITORY-HEV-PIN`. Durable negatives mutate the real macOS archive, its lock, and the missing/extra file set while requiring exit 1 and `productionCompositionPermitted=false`. The corrected full suite passes 31/31, authoritative production validation permits the unchanged baseline at manifest SHA-256 `095372d61bd2baf46a72c701e0b94ee18cece67b8d51d445b30f50e2b5c39c73`, native dependency validation and lint exit 0, and board validation exits 0 while retaining two explicit parent-status mismatch rows. An earlier 30/30 test execution is retained as non-acceptance evidence because its zsh wrapper attempted to assign the read-only `status` variable and therefore exited 1 after the tests.\n48|\n49|### M0 packet bridge and HEV baseline accepted (TASK-260715-2jatnd)\n50|\n51|- DECISION: The macOS-first M0 Bridge gate passes under ADR-024 with MTU 1500, requested/effective 32768-byte socket buffers, 64-packet/5-ms pump budgets, HEV task-stack/TCP/UDP-copy values 24576/4096/2, and an initial measured 500-session ceiling. MTU 4096 remains injectable behind end-to-end proof; MTU 8500 is a named failed default candidate because constrained-buffer rows produced 768 `EMSGSIZE` refusals.\n52|- EVIDENCE: Public-API bridge, IPv4/IPv6 framing, deterministic faults, hostile-frame fuzzing, bounded pressure, lifecycle, packaging, notices, and physical Mac MTU/memory rows trace to accepted task-scoped resources in `docs/TASK-260715-2jatnd_m0-bridge-hev-decision-adr.md`. The worst measured incremental HEV/bridge cost was 9,715,712 bytes at 500 sessions; whole-extension SSH/DNS/relay/cache/reconnect memory remains unmeasured.\n53|- FORK: Unmodified HEV `ad7600497931205105b08367bd1b450048157e40` remains selected. There is no Instruments material-bottleneck evidence or improved callback prototype, so fork authorization is rejected by default.\n54|- GAPS: Physical iPhone, jetsam, NAT64, sleep/wake, energy, and whole-extension memory stay deferred/blocked and are never inferred from macOS. M1 consumers may use this baseline only through the exact accepted-outcome binding task; production composition remains fail-closed on its other M0 inputs.\n55|\n56|## 2026-08-27\n57|\n58|### 0824 — Deterministic lifecycle release is separated from Darwin residency (TASK-260715-135rr8)\n59|- ROOT CAUSE: The rev3 gate coupled two distinct claims. Harness-owned channels, reads, queues, boundary/native lifecycle counters, and descriptor-close stages were released deterministically, but the gate still required Darwin to return allocator pages during the same 500-cycle observation. One reviewer run therefore failed on a bounded `+180224 B` non-decreasing footprint rise while the next two passed.\n60|- FIX: The schema-2 lifecycle evidence now records an `ownedResourcesReleased` verdict and every underlying ownership field for each cycle. The production gate requires 500 cycles, zero failed owned-resource-release cycles, and the unchanged post-warmup maximum resident-footprint drawup ceiling of `256 KiB`. Monotonic and late rises remain classified with exact transition/cycle evidence, but OS page-return timing is no longer used as a proxy for product-owned release. The matrix atomically emits its exact-tree-bound raw report before returning a failing verdict.\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
